"Compares corresponding quantiles from two distributions to reveal location, scale, and shape differences.\n\nUse when: A sample needs comparison with a theoretical family or another sample. Tail departures and distributional shape matter more than histogram bin choices.\n\nAvoid when: The comparison requires a probability scale or censoring has not been handled correctly.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes reference-distribution or second-sample quantiles. Vertical position encodes corresponding sample quantiles on the same probability grid.\n\nBuild: Sort values and choose a stated plotting-position convention. Calculate matching reference quantiles or interpolate second-sample quantiles. Draw paired quantiles with a clearly defined equality or fitted location-scale reference line.\n\nCheck: Check probability positions avoid undefined extreme theoretical quantiles. Verify the reference line’s meaning is stated. Identify transformations, fitted distribution parameters, and sample sizes.\n\nPitfalls: A fitted straight reference line can hide location or scale differences deliberately removed by fitting. Tail points are variable in small samples, so do not treat every departure as proof of a wrong family.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic model residual quantiles with normal-theory quantiles, labeling a fitted reference line and the residual sample size.\n\nAlternatives: P–P plot, Histogram, ECDF."
